  "query_preview": "..Isolated storms have begun to form over the White\nMountains and Chiricahuas and in Santa Cruz County. These will\nbe the favored areas for thunderstorm development over the next\ncouple of hours. Aloft, high pressure is centered over the Four\nCorners and Rocky Mountains keeping southeast Arizona under \nmoist easterly flow. GOES total precipitable water ranges from \n1.6\" in western Pima County to 1.25\" along the AZ/NM border,\nwhich is 25-50 percent above normal. Mid-level flow is elevated with\nwinds at 500 mb between 20-30 kts. This will help lead to 0-6 \nkm wind shear values between 15-30 kts this afternoon and \nevening across southeast Arizona. SBCAPE values are around 750 \nJ/kg and and will likely reach as high as 1500 J/kg for portions\nof southeast Arizona. Low levels are also dry with DCAPE values\naround 1500 J/kg. All of these ingredients will lead to an \nactive monsoon day with severe wind gusts, hail, heavy rainfall,\nand blowing dust all a threat. The SPC and WPC both have \nsoutheast Arizona under slight risks for severe weather and \nexcessive rainfall.\n\nThis afternoon expect further development over the higher \nterrain south and east of Tucson with storms moving into the \nlower elevations by mid to late afternoon. As this happens there\nwill be a severe wind gust and blowing dust threat, especially \nnear dust prone areas along I-10 east of Tucson. By late \nafternoon/early evening CAMs show convection mainly outflow \ndriven with storms over eastern Pima County and southeast Pinal \nCounty. However there are a couple HREF members that show \nconvection over the Tucson area by mid-afternoon, so a slight \nchance storms begin earlier than this evening for Tucson. Severe\nwind gusts and blowing dust will continue to be a threat as \nstorms progress westward. Also, with the wind shear in place \nstorms with stronger and possibly rotating updrafts will be \ncapable of hail and wind gusts in excess of 70 mph. The heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding threat will cover most of southeast \nArizona, but the best chances for heavy rainfall will be over \nSanta Cruz County and southwest Cochise County. Storms in these \nareas will be capable of 1\"-2\" an hour and some of these areas \nhave already received rainfall over the past few days.\n\nStorms are expected to dissipate by midnight with skies clearing\nout overnight into the morning. Atmospheric conditions will will\nbe similar Sunday to today with the main difference being breezy\neasterly surface winds along the AZ/NM border. This area does \ndry out some with dewpoints dropping to around 50 degrees and \nPWAT around an inch. However the rest of the area remains moist.\nCAMs are showing a slightly earlier start tomorrow for most of \nthe area with initiation around noon or 1 PM from Tucson \neastward, excluding eastern Cochise County. By mid-afternoon hi-\nres guidance shows a broken line of storms from the Mogollon \nRim down to Nogales. These storms will progress westward through\nthe late afternoon and evening, potentially reaching all the \nway to Ajo. The main threats will again be severe wind gusts, \nblowing dust, hail, and heavy rainfall. If storms do organize as\nCAMs are suggesting they could generate a large area of blowing\ndust that moves through Pima, Pinal, and Maricopa Counties. \n\nEarly into mid next week the high moves further north over the\nnorthern Great Plains which decreases 500 mb flow to around 10\nknots by Tuesday. However flow will still be out of the east \nwhich will keep PWAT values 25-50 percent above normal through \nthe work week. Expect daily afternoon and evening thunderstorms \nwith day to day variability in coverage. Deterministic models \nare also hinting at a weak upper level low moving through the \nregion mid-week, which could enhance storm activity. With the \ncenter of the high so far displaced high temperatures will cool \nto a couple degrees below normal Monday into the middle of next \nweek.",
